As the help page shows (see Details), you need to define your own function that returns what you want, and pass that to the FUN= argument.

myFunc <- function(fit) {

  g1.r2.y <- inspect(fit, 'r2')$`1`[[1]]
  g2.r2.y <- inspect(fit, 'r2')$`2`[[1]]

  g3.r2.y <- inspect(fit, 'r2')$`3`[[1]]

  ## calculate differences
  c(g12 = g1.r2.y - g2.r2.y,
    g13 = g1.r2.y - g3.r2.y,
    g23 = g2.r2.y - g3.r2.y)
}
out <- bootstrapLavaan(g1.r2.y, R=10, type="ordinary", FUN = myFunc)

Look at str(out) to see its structure (you will get a bootstrap distribution of each statistic your function returns).  Then you can write code to calculate your 95% percentile CI, for example:

apply(out, 2, quantile, probs = c(.025, .975))

