and to further muddy the waters about ordinal variables.
this chapter demonstrated that if the ordinal response scale had 5 or 6 ordered options, the data behave as if they are continuous. This can make life a lot simpler if you have a long enough set of options that people actually use.

Finney, S. J., & DiStefano, C. (2006). Non-normal and categorical data in structural equation modeling. In G. R. Hancock & R. D. Mueller (Eds.), Structural equation modeling: A second course (pp. 269-314). Greenwich, CT: Information Age Publishing.

